---
import '../../../styles/pages/legal.css';

import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const alternateLanguages = [
  { lang: 'it', url: 'https://www.retro-gamers.it/risorse/' },
  { lang: 'en', url: 'https://www.retro-gamers.it/en/resources/' },
  { lang: 'x-default', url: 'https://www.retro-gamers.it/risorse/' }
];

const languageSwitch = {
  url: '/risorse/'
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Recommended resources"
  description="Websites, archives, communities and projects worth following for retro gaming, homebrew releases and videogame history."
  image="/og-image.jpg"
  canonical="https://www.retro-gamers.it/en/resources/"
  lang="en"
  languageSwitch={languageSwitch}
  alternateLanguages={alternateLanguages}
>
  <div class="legal-page">
    <div class="container">

      <section class="legal-hero">
        <span>Resources</span>
        <h1>Recommended resources</h1>
        <p>
          Websites, archives, communities and projects we follow or consider useful for anyone
          interested in retro gaming, homebrew releases and videogame history.
        </p>
      </section>

      <article class="legal-content">
        <p>
          Retro-Gamers.it does not exist in isolation. Retro culture is also made of websites,
          archives, forums, communities, social pages and independent projects that collect news,
          preserve memory, share materials and keep the scene alive.
        </p>

        <p>
          This page collects some resources we recommend to readers who want to explore retro
          gaming, follow new releases for classic machines, join technical discussions or discover
          projects close to the spirit of this site.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is not a sponsored list. Links are selected because we consider them useful,
          interesting or consistent with the way we tell videogame history.
        </p>

        <h2>News and homebrew scene</h2>

        <h3><a href="https://www.indieretronews.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Indie Retro News</a></h3>
        <p>
          One of the most followed international websites for indie and retro news, homebrew
          releases, ports, remakes and new projects for systems such as Amiga, Commodore 64,
          ZX Spectrum, Amstrad CPC, MSX and PC. A valuable resource for following the living
          retro-gaming scene, not only its historical memory.
        </p>

        <h2>Friendly sites and Italian projects</h2>

        <h3><a href="https://amigapage.it/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AmigaPage.it</a></h3>
        <p>
          A long-running Italian website dedicated to the Amiga world, with news, articles,
          images, forum activity and materials related to the Commodore machine and its community.
          A project run by Marco Lovera, who has helped keep Amiga culture alive in Italy for
          many years.
        </p>

        <h2>Communities and pages to follow</h2>

      <h3>
  <a href="https://www.facebook.com/groups/628229514520648" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
    AMIGA 4 EVER!
  </a>
</h3>
<p>
  A Facebook group dedicated to the Amiga world, with images, memories, discussions, updates and content shared by enthusiasts. A useful community for following the living, everyday side of Amiga memory: nostalgia, hardware, software, curiosities and dialogue between users.
</p>

        <h2>Forums, archives and technical discussions</h2>

        <h3><a href="https://eab.abime.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">English Amiga Board</a></h3>
        <p>
          One of the most important international forums for the Amiga community. A useful
          resource for technical discussions, support, preservation, hardware, software and deeper
          exploration of the Amiga ecosystem.
        </p>

        <h2>Marketplaces and retro material</h2>

        <h3><a href="https://www.amibay.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AmiBay</a></h3>
        <p>
          A forum and marketplace focused on buying and selling retro hardware and material, with
          a strong Amiga presence. A practical resource for anyone looking for components,
          expansions, machines, spare parts or collectible material, always with the normal care
          required for private transactions.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
